{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"The condition appears to have three regimes: equality holds when the package value is at most the smallest winner value, when the winners’ total value minus the package value is at most the smallest winner value, or when all three winner values are equal. I also found a simple counterexample: with values \\((v_P,v_A,v_B,v_C)=(3,1,2,3)\\), the nearest point is \\((1,1,1)\\), while the centroid is \\((1/2,1,3/2)\\). I’m checking the proof and zero-value cases with opus.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '\\\\n[sol] 2026-10-22: Proof complete: translate p=l+y, ui=min(vi,D), s=sum ui-D. Pair ui+uj>=s means corner cuts of side di=(s-ui)+ disjoint. Centroid yi=[s\"'^3-sum dj''^3-3 ui di''^2]/[3(s''^2-sum dj''^2)]. Equality to VN iff centroid uniform iff ui di''^2 constant. For positive ui, this means all di=0 or all ui equal: sorted a<c with a+c>=s imply f(a)>f(c) for f(t)=t(s-t)''^2 when both cuts positive (if a<s/3 compare c>=s-a; otherwise monotone). Zero winner => segment, midpoint always VN. Excellent example P=3, vals1,2,3: F is parallelogram parameterized pA in[0,1],pB in[0,2],pC=3-pA-pB; centroid(.5,1,1.5), VN(1,1,1). Ready to draft answer.md pending your validation.'\"\\\\n' >> /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
